A||||28||gtcash||Auto Trans with V8 swap?||||6||
Z||000000||gtcash||09-28-2001||07:40 PM||||My clutch foot is tired...can I put an automatic trans next to my Chev 350? I have a spare tranny from an 86 SE. Will it get tore up or what?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||Leper||09-28-2001||08:37 PM||||Yes you can.||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||09-28-2001||10:22 PM||||You should create a new post titled "Hey Archie, how's the beefed auto doing???"<P>Archie knows a guy in Michigan that will take your auto and modify with new parts to hold up to the V8 (charges about $1,000 to modify trans).  He should have some drag test results with the beefed auto pretty soon that had a high horse V8 attached to it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87FieroGTx||09-28-2001||10:51 PM|||| Would the trans from a 3800s/c fit? 4speed auto.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gtcash||09-28-2001||11:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leper:<BR><B>Yes you can.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hey, Leper,<BR>You know first hand? Have you done it?<BR>Do you use Archie's adapter? What about the flywheel vs Flex plate?<BR>A reply of "yes you can" is glib.<BR>How about a little insight...<BR>I realize anything is possible given enough time and money...<BR>Let's get practical, is an auto practical?<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000005||dennis_6||09-28-2001||11:26 PM||||I would put an caddy 400t4 auto tranny in there.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bazooka||09-29-2001||06:56 PM||||Cal Kid,<BR>    I've had a ($1200) Beef up Job done to my 125c Trans by Archie and JPT Transmissions in Michigan. Its been rebuilt 5 times so far by Archies builder. It just won't hold up to a V8 conversion.<BR> <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>*******************************<BR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR>[B]You should create a new post titled "Hey Archie, how's the beefed auto doing???"<P>Archie knows a guy in Michigan that will take your auto and modify with new parts to hold up to the V8 (charges about $1,000 to modify trans).  He should have some drag test results with the beefed auto pretty soon that had a high horse V8 attached to it<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||California Kid||09-29-2001||08:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bazooka:<BR><B>Cal Kid,<BR>    I've had a ($1200) Beef up Job done to my 125c Trans by Archie and JPT Transmissions in Michigan. Its been rebuilt 5 times so far by Archies builder. It just won't hold up to a V8 conversion.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Bazooka,<BR>Thanks for posting your experiences with the "Beefed Auto", sorry to hear it didn't work out. I guess that may be why Archie didn't post a follow-up with results from a post he made about 2 months back.<P>Are you following any other leads for putting a stronger automatic in your car? I still think an auto trans with a tricked "hard shift" valve body, and 3500 stall converter would be a great setup for the V8's, if you could get it to live.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||cardealer||09-29-2001||11:10 PM||||my 125 works great behind my v8. i figure i have around 360h.p. (at the crank) plus a 125 shot of n20. i had my tranny rebuilt when i did my conversion by a local transmisson shop. granted i have put less than 5000 miles on mine(but alot of drag racing and hard driveing), it shifts very good. if you have your tranny rebuilt you should spend the money and get a brand NEW front pump, a NEW tork converter, and a NEW trans oil cooler. the guy that did the rebuild for me really stressed that those componets are the most likely to cause your tranny to fail prematurely. i change my tranny filter and fluid every time i change my engine oil.<BR>just some thoughts.||||reg||6
Z||000009||Bazooka||09-30-2001||12:06 AM||||Cal Kid,<BR>   I have a new 3400 Stallspeed Torque Converter, Pump, Valve Body, Shift Kit, Ceramic and Kevlar replacement parts in the Clutch Packs and Bands, 28000 GVW Cooler and I still have to drive my ZZ4 with a lot of reservation in that I DO try to avoid mashing down on the accelerator with street driving and no dragstrips (1/4 mile) just to extend the life of the trans. I've checked various trans builders and none will even warrantee the Fiero SBC's because of the torque. I was thinking of getting an extra 125c that could be built and put in the next time that it wears out (or blows). I'd like to know how you can get such fast E.T's with your Fiero (low 12's if I remember the previous posts). I'm still trying to find a builder that can help but no luck yet.  <BR>******************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by Bazooka:<BR>[b]Cal Kid,<BR>    I've had a ($1200) Beef up Job done to my 125c Trans by Archie and JPT Transmissions in Michigan. Its been rebuilt 5 times so far by Archies builder. It just won't hold up to a V8 conversion.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Bazooka,<BR>Thanks for posting your experiences with the "Beefed Auto", sorry to hear it didn't work out. I guess that may be why Archie didn't post a follow-up with results from a post he made about 2 months back.<P>Are you following any other leads for putting a stronger automatic in your car? I still think an auto trans with a tricked "hard shift" valve body, and 3500 stall converter would be a great setup for the V8's, if you could get it to live.<BR>*********************************<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||California Kid||09-30-2001||08:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bazooka:<BR><B>Cal Kid,<BR>   I'd like to know how you can get such fast E.T's with your Fiero (low 12's if I remember the previous posts). I'm still trying to find a builder that can help but no luck yet.  <BR>******************************<BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Standard 5 spd Getrag with QuarterMaster Double Disk Clutch Setup, race application not intended for street. Clutch Disks only have a life of about 10,000 miles, but they don't slip at all. Engine is estimated at 400 to 420 hp with torque around 390 ft. lbs.<P>Reason I am curious about auto trans is that it would absorb some of the shock loading to the CV joints/half shafts. I Haven't broken one in two years now, but know they are the weakest link in my setup. My cousin in LA wants to Convert a GT with a old Pontiac 389 V8 tripower stepup w/modified auto, that's the main reason I'm paying attention to these trans strings.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Bazooka||09-30-2001||08:52 PM||||Cal Kid,<BR>   There have been several successful longitudinal V8 conversions done with the TH-325 and TH-425 using BBC engines in the Fiero. These tranny's would offer more versatilty with engine HP. S. Joe Wyman has prepared an Equipment list and what parts to use. If you care to inquire, he's at Associated Auto in Hackensack, N.J. and his phone is (201) 457-9502. It may be possible to use a 389 or even a 421 with tri=power this way. Varooom !<BR>*********************************<BR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR>Standard 5 spd Getrag with QuarterMaster Double Disk Clutch Setup, race application not intended for street. Clutch Disks only have a life of about 10,000 miles, but they don't slip at all. Engine is estimated at 400 to 420 hp with torque around 390 ft. lbs.<P>Reason I am curious about auto trans is that it would absorb some of the shock loading to the CV joints/half shafts. I Haven't broken one in two years now, but know they are the weakest link in my setup. My cousin in LA wants to Convert a GT with a old Pontiac 389 V8 tripower stepup w/modified auto, that's the main reason I'm paying attention to these trans strings.<BR>*****************************||||reg||1
Z||000012||Archie||09-30-2001||09:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bazooka:<BR><B>Cal Kid,<BR>    I've had a ($1200) Beef up Job done to my 125c Trans by Archie and JPT Transmissions in Michigan. Its been rebuilt 5 times so far by Archies builder. It just won't hold up to a V8 conversion.<BR> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've just returned from a little vacation and find that I've been missed here. I have about 1400 EMails to sort thru and about 10 days of Pennocks to review. So forgive me if I try to make this brief.<P>I would like to point out just a couple of minor items here.<P>Marty's transmission has been re-built a total of 3 times (including the original build) by myself and JPT. When we did Martys original V-8 swap we had his transmission re-built as a part of that process. Then 2 times after that, when Marty said it wasn't working right we completely R&R'ed the COMPLETE system for him at ABSOLUTELY NO CHARGE to him. <P>Until a post made to this Forum about 3 months ago by Marty, I had never heard from him that the last R&R we did was not satisfactory. At that same time I heard that he had taken us (myself & JPT) out of the loop on this thing by going to and paying another transmission shop to re-build it at least a couple more times.<P>So, the best as I can understand, Marty may have had his Transmission R&R'ed 5 times. But after the original assembly of the car, we (myself & JPT) only R&R'ed the car TWICE (not 5 times). I/We Did not charge Marty for parts & labor for any of the extra R&R work we did. And we never questioned him on a driving style that could have contributed to it's demise. The transmission shop he had work on the car after we had it last, has charged him up the ass and evidently failed to build a satisfactory transmission. <BR>However, they do not have to take responsibility for their work, they can just claim "It can't be done" and Marty turns around and blames even the other guy's failures on me and the lowly 125.<P>I have quite a few customers out there running around in V-8 Fieros using JPT 125 automatic transmissions (and transmissions built by other shops) and they are happy. I know that they can work and work fine. There are way too many 125 success stories to just ignore them and site on this one case of problems.<P>If there was an inherent problem with 125's beefed-up and put into a V-8 Fiero, then why in the heck would I build turn key V-8 Automatic swaps for customers in house, when it results in me having to redo the job for free a couple of extra times??????<P>Considering the history of many people successfully using beefed-up 125's in V-8 Fieros. I think that, We/I have gone above and beyond the call to try to make this a happy outcome. <P>The last transmission that we put in Marty's car (before the other guy worked on it) was one of three transmissions I had JPT do for me all at once. Another one of that group of transmissions is the one I just put in a car I refer to as my drag car. We figure that the engine in that car is 400+ HP. We have yet to take that car to the strip because we need to put on better brakes. We've been practicing hole shots out in front of the shop and It Kicks A$$. Can't wait to get it to the strip. When it's ready (whenever I have time to finish it) it will be ran thru the ringer and you all will be the first to hear the results.<P>I hope this helps.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||09-30-2001||09:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtcash:<BR><B>My clutch foot is tired...can I put an automatic trans next to my Chev 350? I have a spare tranny from an 86 SE. Will it get tore up or what?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Our Stick and Automatic V-8 kits are not interchangeable. The Stick/Automatic adapter plates and the FLywheel/Flexplate situation are designs that are unique to the type of kit you have.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000014||dantheman1998||09-30-2001||10:18 PM||||I am kinda new. I want to put a V8 in my fiero. So from what i understand the th-125 will snap under stress? Or will the TH-125 handle it? i plan on doing alot of Street-strip abuse. I just want to know if the th-125 will handle it?||||reg||1
Z||000015||dantheman1998||09-30-2001||10:24 PM||||can a 454 fit after putting a longitude TH-325 trans?||||reg||1
Z||000016||dennis_6||09-30-2001||10:30 PM||||Welcome to the forum!!!<BR>Archie is the man to talk to, but from what I have heard a TH-125 can take a mild v8 if the transmission is built up.  I would not try a stock TH-125.  Caddilac 4 speed auto's are also an low cost alternative from what I understand.<BR>Hope this helps! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||dennis_6||09-30-2001||10:36 PM||||Anything can work.  The longitudal set up is not the best way to put an engine in.  You lose the trunk and as far as I know there are no kits for that set up.  You could put a 454 in a Fiero it has been done.  You lose most of the cars reliability, very poor gas mileage.  It will not be a daily driver and the torque will be hard on the cv joints.  Forget that on a TH-125.  A 350 can be built to incredible horsepower anyways.  A 454 longitudal setup is a bad ideal in my humble opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Alex4mula||10-01-2001||11:40 AM||||gtcash; I guess you have your answer. Will need to keep sticking  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Bring that car to the club meeting pleeease  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||Bazooka||10-01-2001||11:49 AM||||*******************************<P>5 times I've returned my Fiero to Archie for Trans Failure in the 125c that he rebuilt. He held on to it for 3 to 6 months each time and the last time he told me "that was all he could do". Why wouldn't I want to get it taken care of in a timely fashion so that I can drive it? Roadmasters Transmissions rebuilt my trans ONCE after that and I am still using their rebuilt trans today. When the last JPT trans was taken apart by 'Roadmasters', it had replacement parts from several different rebuilders in it as well as faulty major parts (torque converter, valve body, bands, clutch packs). So basically, there was nothing much left to reuse. When I contracted Archie for the V8 conversion, I discussed how I intended to use the car. There was no problem fulfilling my request at that time. I didn't specify a new ZZ4 in my car just to go to church and my 'Driving Habits' weren't an issue then. I've owned this 86 Fiero GT since 1986 and it is always garaged, protected and babied. I woudn't return my car to Archies shop for the small complaints but there should still be a responsibility for major parts that are installed to be WORKING PROPERLY (transmision, fuel injection/carb, electrical, exhaust, cooling syst.). The 5 JPT transmissions all failed within 2 days to 4 months time.           <BR>***************************                <BR>***************************<BR>Originally posted by Archie: Marty's transmission has been re-built a total of 3 times (including the original build) by myself and JPT. When we did Martys original V-8 swap we had his transmission re-built as a part of that process. Then 2 times after that, when Marty said it wasn't working right we completely R&R'ed the COMPLETE system for him at ABSOLUTELY NO CHARGE to him. <BR>Until a post made to this Forum about 3 months ago by Marty, I had never heard from him that the last R&R we did was not satisfactory. At that same time I heard that he had taken us (myself & JPT) out of the loop on this thing by going to and paying another transmission shop to re-build it at least a couple more times.<BR>So, the best as I can understand, Marty may have had his Transmission R&R'ed 5 times. But after the original assembly of the car, we (myself & JPT) only R&R'ed the car TWICE (not 5 times). I/We Did not charge Marty for parts & labor for any of the extra R&R work we did. And we never questioned him on a driving style that could have contributed to it's demise. The transmission shop he had work on the car after we had it last, has charged him up the ass and evidently failed to build a satisfactory transmission. <BR>However, they do not have to take responsibility for their work, they can just claim "It can't be done" and Marty turns around and blames even the other guy's failures on me and the lowly 125.<BR>I have quite a few customers out there running around in V-8 Fieros using JPT 125 automatic transmissions (and transmissions built by other shops) and they are happy. I know that they can work and work fine. There are way too many 125 success stories to just ignore them and site on this one case of problems.<BR>If there was an inherent problem with 125's beefed-up and put into a V-8 Fiero, then why in the heck would I build turn key V-8 Automatic swaps for customers in house, when it results in me having to redo the job for free a couple of extra times??????<BR>Considering the history of many people successfully using beefed-up 125's in V-8 Fieros. I think that, We/I have gone above and beyond the call to try to make this a happy outcome. <BR>Archie<BR>*******************************||||reg||1
Z||000020||rgeeinc||10-01-2001||01:42 PM||||In reference to Archies transmission, I was there and watched the said hole shots in front of the shop. Archie was just finishing up my car,( the reason I was at the shop). As Archie put it, they were some A$$ kicking burnouts. That car really runs, and no problems with the transmission, in fact I am considering using a automatic in my next V8 conversion. Hope this helps.<P>  I just put in a car I refer to as my drag car. We figure that the engine in that car is 400+ HP. We have yet to take that car to the strip because we need to put on better brakes. We've been practicing hole shots out in front of the shop and It Kicks A$$. Can't wait to get it to the strip. When it's ready (whenever I have time to finish it) it will be ran thru the ringer and you all will be the first to hear the results.<P>I hope this helps.<P>Archie<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by rgeeinc (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000021||dantheman1998||10-02-2001||09:54 PM||||Can a TH-125 handle like 600 hp? or more? What else can i use to handle this kind of power. And i dont care about trunk space or driveabiley or gasmileage because this will not be a everyday car anyway. This will be my drag car and something to bring to he shows. Do the axles snap?<P>A couple of things i want to know about this car.<P>How much does it wiegh?<P>And one last thing. Who in the right mind would care about trunk space in a fiero. Theres like none in there with a 4 cylinder.<BR>Thats like asking if a honda CRX has head space. Its just dumb.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Mach10||10-03-2001||12:17 AM||||Archie: Exactly WHAT is done to the trannies to "beef them up" for 1000? This is out of curiosity. I'm not sure that a v8 is right for me, but it's still a fascinating swap. I know your instructions by heart, but have NO idea what you do to the 125c...||||reg||5
Z||000023||gtcash||10-03-2001||07:45 AM|||| Anybody want to swap the V8 stick kit parts for the V8 automatic kit parts? I kinda believe Archie, actually I have the balls to try it myself!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by gtcash (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||1FST2M6||10-03-2001||10:00 AM||||LOL.. uum "can it handle 600hp" in the unlikey event you even brake 500hp at the wheels  i'm sure you'll have traction isses that far out weight the trannys ability to handle more hp... having driven a 350 laiden 84 4spd.. with only 250lbft at the ground thats more than enough to both get you in trouble, put you into a wall faster than you can stop and get plenty of smokey burn-out awards from the local law enforcement. <P>mime handles over 300lbft and shock loads of over 400lbft evertime i'm spraying.. the input pump is mad at me but it still runs/drives like it alwasy has... HP doesnt mean much.. the off idle torque and your ability to not beat the crap out of the car at every light will dictate weither or not the tranny will last. <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91||||reg||1
Z||000025||dennis_6||10-03-2001||01:52 PM||||The Fiero's trunk space is not a dumb concern.  A Fiero has a decent amount of trunk spave it is just vertical rather than horizontal.  Most people dont like cutting into the trunk. Before you go bashing people explain what you want. <P>As for the longitudal set up go here: <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||dantheman1998||10-03-2001||03:39 PM||||i want a car that will be in the 10 seconds or lower. I need a tranny that wont give me trouble. I am not a handling freak and i dont plan on going to the supermarket to fill the trunk with groceries. I am willing to do anything to make a bullit proof tranny to handle the hp or tourqe i want.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||California Kid||10-03-2001||07:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dantheman1998:<BR><B>i want a car that will be in the 10 seconds or lower. I need a tranny that wont give me trouble. I am not a handling freak and i dont plan on going to the supermarket to fill the trunk with groceries. I am willing to do anything to make a bullit proof tranny to handle the hp or tourqe i want.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>A Company in Europe will make you a trans for the Fiero that will hold 600hp, only one problem, the price is about 20,000 dollars. They specialize in race car transmission fabrications up to and including Formula 1. If you're interested I can get you the name of the Company.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||rockcrawl||10-03-2001||08:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>i want a car that will be in the 10 seconds or lower. I need a tranny that wont give me trouble. I am not a handling freak and i dont plan on going to the supermarket to fill the trunk with groceries. I am willing to do anything to make a bullit proof tranny to handle the hp or tourqe i want<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>It sounds like you want a TH425 mounted bacwards.||||reg||1
